property_value: foaf:depiction "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a9/Subtalar_Joint.PNG" xsd:anyURI
property_value: RO:0002175 NCBITaxon:9606
property_value: UBPROP:0000008 "The talus is apparently derived from the fusion of three separate bones in the feet of primitive amphibians; the tibiale, articulating with tibia, the intermedium, between the bases of the tibia and fibula, and the fourth centrale, lying in the mid-part of the tarsus. These bones are still partially separate in modern amphibians, which therefore do not have a true talus. The talus forms a considerably more flexible joint in mammals than it does in reptiles. This reaches its greatest extent in artiodactyls, where the distal surface of the bone has a smooth keel to allow greater freedom of movement of the foot, and thus increase running speed" xsd:string {source="WP"}

